Q: CQRS asynchronously command bus vs synchronously A: <blockquote>
<p>I don't like the use of a asynchronous command bus ... I.e you can't tell the user if the command succeeded or failed.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>use <strong>event bus</strong> instead... in Event-Driven Architecture:
components of event-driven architecture include:</p>
<ul>
<li>Events: Immutable messages representing a change or an occurrence in the system.</li>
<li>Event producers: Services that generate and publish events.</li>
<li>Event consumers: Services that subscribe to events and react accordingly.</li>
<li>Message brokers: Middleware (e.g., RabbitMQ queues, Kafka) responsible for routing and delivering events between producers and consumers.</li>
</ul>
<p>So, if your commandHandler (in Write Service) will publish events (with some json structure), your Read Service being subscribed for it will get these Notifications to be updated then - if you already <a href="https://microservices.io/refactoring/index.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">refactored</a> your code to these 2 microservices.
If you have 1 DB(storage) - you can still subscribe for the event <a href="https://medium.com/@mokarchi/building-a-custom-event-bus-in-net-24b9e195c57d" rel="nofollow noreferrer">in the same manner</a> - with event bus:</p>
<pre><code>//code from link
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class InMemoryEventBus : IEventBus
{
    private readonly Dictionary&lt;Type, List&lt;Delegate&gt;&gt; _handlers = new();

    public void Publish&lt;T&gt;(T @event) where T : class
    {
        var eventType = typeof(T);
        if (_handlers.ContainsKey(eventType))
        {
            foreach (var handler in _handlers[eventType])
            {
                ((Action&lt;T&gt;)handler)(@event);
            }
        }
    }

    public void Subscribe&lt;T&gt;(Action&lt;T&gt; handler) where T : class
    {
        var eventType = typeof(T);
        if (!_handlers.ContainsKey(eventType))
        {
            _handlers[eventType] = new List&lt;Delegate&gt;();
        }
        _handlers[eventType].Add(handler);
    }
}

</code></pre>
<p>You will get published notification when it will occur</p>

Q: Intersecting Multiple times against the same table (set) on JOIN A: <p>A small seed to test on <a href="https://stackoverflow.com">https://onecompiler.com/oracle</a></p>
<pre><code>-- 1. Setup Tables
CREATE TABLE Account (
    id NUMBER PRIMARY KEY,
    zip_code_id NUMBER
);

CREATE TABLE Package (
    id NUMBER PRIMARY KEY,
    name VARCHAR2(50)
);

CREATE TABLE Account_Package (
    account_id NUMBER,
    package_id NUMBER,
    PRIMARY KEY (account_id, package_id)
);

CREATE TABLE Special (
    id NUMBER PRIMARY KEY,
    name VARCHAR2(50),
    zip_code_id NUMBER
);

CREATE TABLE Package_Special (
    package_id NUMBER,
    special_id NUMBER,
    PRIMARY KEY (package_id, special_id)
);

-- 2. Seed Data
-- Account 1 is in Zip 90210
INSERT INTO Account (id, zip_code_id) VALUES (1, 90210);

-- Packages
INSERT INTO Package (id, name) VALUES (10, 'Gold Internet');
INSERT INTO Package (id, name) VALUES (20, 'Silver TV');

-- Link Account 1 to Gold Internet (ID 10)
INSERT INTO Account_Package (account_id, package_id) VALUES (1, 10);

-- Specials
-- Special 1: Correct Zip, Linked to Gold Internet (SHOULD MATCH)
INSERT INTO Special (id, name, zip_code_id) VALUES (100, 'Welcome Gold Promo', 90210);
INSERT INTO Package_Special (package_id, special_id) VALUES (10, 100);

-- Special 2: Correct Zip, Linked to Silver TV (SHOULD NOT MATCH - Account doesn't have Silver TV)
INSERT INTO Special (id, name, zip_code_id) VALUES (200, 'Silver TV Discount', 90210);
INSERT INTO Package_Special (package_id, special_id) VALUES (20, 200);

-- Special 3: WRONG Zip, Linked to Gold Internet (SHOULD NOT MATCH)
INSERT INTO Special (id, name, zip_code_id) VALUES (300, 'Gold Out-of-State', 55555);
INSERT INTO Package_Special (package_id, special_id) VALUES (10, 300);

</code></pre>
<p>Now the query using Multi-Condition JOIN approach:</p>
<pre><code>SELECT DISTINCT s.id, s.name, s.zip_code_id
FROM Account a
INNER JOIN Account_Package ap ON a.id = ap.account_id
INNER JOIN Package_Special ps ON ap.package_id = ps.package_id
INNER JOIN Special s ON ps.special_id = s.id 
    AND a.zip_code_id = s.zip_code_id
WHERE a.id = 1;
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Why this works where your original failed?</strong></p>
<p>In your original query, you joined Special twice using two different aliases (by_location_special and by_package_special).</p>
<p>In SQL, if you join a table twice, the database treats them as two completely unrelated sets of data. It would find any special that matched the zip, and then separately find any special that matched the package, often resulting in a &quot;Cartesian product&quot; (every possible combination of the two) which makes your results look like a mess.</p>
<p>By using one JOIN with an AND condition, you are forcing the database to find a single row in the Special table that satisfies both requirements simultaneously.</p>

Q: Javascript assigning value to variable A: <p>IMPORTANT: All JavaScript developers should know this. It will cause all kinds of weird bugs that is very hard to find.</p>

<p>It is a common mistake of people who are new to JavaScript. I've made the same mistake before.</p>

<p>A function inside a loop is NOT created for every iteration. It is the same one function object with the same closure scope. Thus, your cells will have the exact same onclick callback.</p>

<p>My advice here is NEVER EVER create a function inside of loop. Instead, create and call a function that returns a callback function and assign it to onclick.</p>

<pre><code>for (var j = 0; j &lt; 8; j++) {
    var cell = row.insertCell(j);
    cell.name = j;
    cell.onclick = createOnClick(cell);
}

function createOnClick(cell) {
    return function () {
        // do whatever you want to do with cell
    };
}
</code></pre>
